evel function:\n\n```python\ndef _positive_int_or_none(v: str | None, minimum: int = 32) -> int | None:\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    try:\n        value = int(v)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        raise ValueError(f\"invalid int value {v!r}, must be an integer >= {minimum}\") from None\n    if value < minimum:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{value}, must be >= {minimum}\")\n    return value\n```\n\nThe descriptor's __get__ wraps exception with f\"{self.name}: {e}\", so the error message will contain var name. Good.\n\nNote descriptor __get__ wrap: `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. ValueError(message) - fine.\n\nEmpty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError → wrapped. Good.\n\nAlso add factory method `int_or_none_min` to EnvVar? Optional. I'll add a factory for clarity:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int = 32) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        return EnvVar(lambda v: _int_or_none_min(v, minimum))\n```\n\nKeep it simple — add both module helper and factory.\n\nlogger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only `max_message_chars`.\n\n```python\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be >= 32, got {max_message_chars}\")\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nCareful: isinstance check - for non-int like \"64\" raise ValueError. Also float 64.0 → not int → ValueError. Task says \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\". OK.\n\nformat method: need to truncate the message body only, before prefixing. Approach: compute message with args substituted without mutating record. Use `record.getMessage()` which does msg % args without mutation. But exc_info text and stack_info appended by super().format(). We need: truncate only the message, then format with prefix, keeping exception text.\n\nSimplest: in format(), build the truncated message by creating a shallow copy of the record? That may break other handlers? Actually a shallow copy via `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)` or copy.copy(record) is safe: original untouched. But the multi-line prefix logic uses `record.message` after super().format(). Better: do truncation on a copy, then call super().format(copy) — the copy's message gets formatted, and record.message attribute set on copy? logging.Formatter.format sets `record.message = record.getMessage()`. That's a mutation of record (setting message attr). Hmm, that's acceptable? Original code already calls super().format(record) which sets record.message. Task says don't rewrite msg, args, exc_info, stack_info. So we can operate like this:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        budget = self.max_message_chars\n        if budget is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        else:\n            full_message = record.getMessage()  # args already substituted, no mutation\n            truncated = _truncate_message(full_message, budget)\n            if truncated == full_message:\n                msg = super().format(record)\n            else:\n                saved = record.msg, record.args  # hmm\n```\n\nBetter approach: temporarily set record.message? Actually logging.Formatter.format does:\n\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): record.asctime = ...\ns = self.formatMessage(record)\nif record.exc_info: ... append\nif record.stack_info: ...\n```\n\nWe can bypass by implementing:\n- compute `text = record.getMessage()`\n- truncated = _truncate(text, budget)\n- then produce output like super().format but with truncated message. To avoid reimplementing, use copy:\n\n```python\nimport copy\n...\nrecord_copy = copy.copy(record)\nrecord_copy.message = truncated\n```\n\nBut super().format(record_copy) will recompute record.message = record_copy.getMessage() which returns msg%args again (full). Unless we set record_copy.msg = truncated, record_copy.args = () — that mutates the copy, not the original. That is allowed: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args...\" — the copy is fine.\n\nSo:\n\n```python\n        if budget is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            text = record.getMessage()\n            clipped = _truncate_message(text, budget)\n            if clipped is not text:\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = clipped\n                record.args = ()\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message:\n            prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]\n            msg = msg.replace(\"\\n\", \"\\n\" + prefix)\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm but the multi-line prefix logic: after truncation with marker, the newline inside? `record.message` is the truncated message. `msg.find(record.message)` works since formatMessage puts record.message in. Fine.\n\nBut careful: The truncation should count on the message after args substitution — yes, `record.getMessage()`. But also should newline counting keep? Yes count newlines as chars.\n\nAlso: exception traceback appended after: super().format appends exc_text. Good — budget only limits message.\n\nWait, `record.getMessage()` for a copy: if our truncated msg contains \"%\" characters, msg % () would fail? logging: if self.args: msg = msg % self.args. args=() is falsy → no formatting. Good.\n\nBut the original code path for non-truncated keeps record as-is.\n\nAlso single-line check `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message` — record.message assigned by super().format on the copy. Fine.\n\n_truncate helper:\n\n```python\n_OMIT_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    marker = _OMIT_MARKER\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message[:max_chars]\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return f\"{message[:head]}{marker}{message[len(message) - tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n```\n\nCheck example: budget 32, marker len 15 (\"...<omitted>...\" — count: 3 dots + \"<omitted>\" 9 chars + 3 dots = 15). remaining = 17. head = 9, tail = 8. matches \"预算32时，保留开头9字符、末尾8字符\". Total = 9+15+8=32. \n\n`_setup_logger` default init reads config:\n\n```python\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n```\n\nNeed import: `from deepinfer.environ import envs` in logger.py. Check existing imports in logger.py — I need lines 1-32. Let me read.\n\nstats_logger.py: find queue consumer and add extra={\"keep_full_message\": True}.\n\nLet me read logger.py lines 1-32 and 108-end, and stats_logger.py full."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8795,"completion_tokens":2291,"total_tokens":11086,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2159},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":4699},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}